    "lawCode": "Amd Part NN §§1 & 2, Chap 54 of 2016; amd §1, Chap 54 of 2017; amd §1, Chap 55 of 2016",
    "lawSection": "Metropolitan Transportation Authority",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6776\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend chapter 54 of the laws of 2016,\namending the public authorities law relating to committing the state\nof New York and the city of New York to partially fund part of the\ncosts of the Metropolitan Transportation Authority's capital program,\nin relation to accelerating the development and installation of\nadvanced signal and communications technologies; and to amend chapter\n54 of the laws of 2017, enacting the capital projects budget and\nchapter 55 of the laws of 2016, enacting the capital projects budget,\nin relation to funding for the acceleration of the development and\ninstallation of advanced signal and communications technologies by the\nmetropolitan transportation authority\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nProvides for the accelerated development and installation of advanced\nsignal and communications technologies by the metropolitan\ntransportation authority.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 provides this bills legislative intent.\n\nSection 2 amends section one and two of Part NN of Chapter 54 of the\nlaws of 2016.\n\nSection 3 amends section one of Chapter 54 of the Laws to 2017.\n\nSection 4 amends section 1 of Chapter 55 of the Laws of 2016, as\namended by Chapter 54, section 1, of the laws of 2017.\n\nSection 5 provides the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe Metropolitan Transportation Authority (MTA) has 837 track miles,\nover 1,600 mainline switches and 13,000 signals. While the three most\nrecent MTA capital plans dedicate more than $5 billion to signal\nrepair and upgrades, much of this funding is used for maintaining\nexisting signaling systems, some of which are more than a century old.\nAs ridership reaches high last seen in the post-World War II years,\nreplacing the signal system must receive the highest priority to\nensure a safe, efficient and reliable means of transportation.\nAlthough the New York City subway system has made strides in recent\nyears in upgrading stations, subway cars and passengers' experience,\none crucial area - signaling - the subway system remains antiquated,\nrelying primarily on century-old technology to keep trains running.\nWhile New York is in the early stages of converting to\ncommunications-based train control, the modern telecommunications\nsystem that many of the world's metro systems rely on today, the pace\nof change has been slow. At the current rate, a full transformation\nwould not occur for more than 50 years, putting the city decades\nbehind its peers around the globe, according to a report completed by\nthe Regional Plan Association in 2014. Additionally, the Citizen's\nBudget Commission recently looked into this matter and found that \"at\nLIRR less than one-third of continuing needs are funded for rolling\nstock and track, and only 40 percent of needs are funded for\ncommunications and signals.\"\nhups://chery.ordresearchital-plan-not-track-need- better-service\n\nThis bill provides that of the State's overall $8.3 billion commitment\nto the 2015-2019 MTA Capital Program, no less than $1.95 billion shall\nbe expended for the costs to accelerate the development and\ninstallation of advanced signal and communication technologies in\n\nconnection with commuter railroad and mass transit systems within the\nMTA's core capital program.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
